@meta
id: 31-08
title: An augroup becomes two API calls
chapter: 31
type: lesson
skills: lua-config
par: 60
minutes: 7
vscode: no equivalent — this is an editor event hook
open: init.lua

@brief
Autocommands are the last part of a vimrc to port, and the part that looks
least like its vimscript. Four lines of `augroup` become two function
calls:

    vim.api.nvim_create_augroup('Trim', { clear = true })
    vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('BufWritePre', { group = ..., pattern = ..., command = ... })

`nvim_create_augroup` returns an id. You keep it in a local and hand it to
every autocommand that belongs in the group, which replaces the
`augroup`/`augroup END` sandwich entirely. `{ clear = true }` is the
`autocmd!` line: it wipes the group before redefining it, so re-sourcing
your config does not stack up four copies of the same hook.

The event name is the first argument. Everything else — `pattern`,
`group`, `once`, `desc`, and either `command` (a string of ex) or
`callback` (a Lua function) — goes in the table. `callback` is the one
that changes how you write these, because the body can be real code
instead of a `:call` into a function defined elsewhere.

Note `[[ ]]`. That is Lua's long-string syntax, and it is the right way to
hold a vim pattern: backslashes inside it mean nothing to Lua, so
`[[%s/\s\+$//e]]` arrives at vim exactly as written. In ordinary quotes
you would have to double every backslash.

The call in front of you is missing its event and its pattern. Fill both
in so it matches the vimrc augroup quoted above it.

This lesson is graded in vim. The Lua is written, not run.

@keys
| `ci'` | change inside the quotes — works on an empty pair too |
| `nvim_create_autocmd(event, opts)` | event first, everything else in the table |

@hints
Two blanks, both empty quote pairs. The vimrc line in the comment above
has both answers in it.
--
`ci'` puts you between the quotes even when there is nothing between them
yet, and it finds the quote pair forward on the line — you do not have to
land the cursor on it.
--
Search for `create_autocmd`, `ci'` the event, then drop two lines, `0`,
and `ci'` the pattern.

@solution
    /create_autocmd<Enter> ci'BufWritePre<Esc> 2j 0 ci'*.ts<Esc> :wq<Enter>

The `0` matters. After the first change the cursor sits at the end of
`BufWritePre`, and `j` keeps the column — two lines down that is past the
quotes, and `ci'` would look forward, find nothing, and quietly do
nothing. Going to column 0 first costs one keystroke and removes the whole
class of failure.

For the record, the same hook written the modern way:

    vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('BufWritePre', {
      group = trim,
      pattern = '*.ts',
      callback = function()
        local pos = vim.api.nvim_win_get_cursor(0)
        vim.cmd([[%s/\s\+$//e]])
        vim.api.nvim_win_set_cursor(0, pos)
      end,
    })

That restores the cursor after the substitution, which the one-line
version does not. You could write that in vimscript too — it is just
harder to read, and harder still to test, and that gap is the honest
argument for Lua config. Not speed. Legibility of the awkward parts.

@start init.lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/init.lua

-- Trim trailing whitespace before writing a TypeScript file.
-- Ported from the vimrc:
--   augroup Trim
--     autocmd!
--     autocmd BufWritePre *.ts %s/\s\+$//e
--   augroup END

local trim = vim.api.nvim_create_augroup('Trim', { clear = true })

vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('', {
  group = trim,
  pattern = '',
  command = [[%s/\s\+$//e]],
})

@expected init.lua
-- ~/.config/nvim/init.lua

-- Trim trailing whitespace before writing a TypeScript file.
-- Ported from the vimrc:
--   augroup Trim
--     autocmd!
--     autocmd BufWritePre *.ts %s/\s\+$//e
--   augroup END

local trim = vim.api.nvim_create_augroup('Trim', { clear = true })

vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('BufWritePre', {
  group = trim,
  pattern = '*.ts',
  command = [[%s/\s\+$//e]],
})
